course: physics
lesson: 22
title: Lenses and eyes
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A lens is a piece of clear glass or plastic with curved sides that bends light on its way through; a lens thick in the middle gathers the light inwards.
explain: Hold a magnifying glass over an ant and the bent light makes the ant look huge, but the same lens can gather sunlight into a tiny burning spot, so it is never pointed at the Sun or anything alive.
explain: Your eye has its own lens that makes a sharp, upside-down picture on the back of the eye, and your brain turns it the right way up.
explain: If an eye is a little too long or too short the picture lands in the wrong place, and glasses bend the light just enough to put it right.
explain: Telescopes and microscopes put lenses or mirrors together to show what our eyes alone cannot: craters on the Moon, or tiny creatures swimming in a drop of pond water.
fact: A magnifying glass is thicker in the middle and bends light inwards, making small things look bigger.
fact: A magnifying glass can focus sunlight into a hot spot that starts fires, so it is never pointed at the Sun or at anything living.
fact: The lens in your eye makes an upside-down picture on the back of the eye, and the brain turns it the right way up.
fact: The pupil, the dark hole in the middle of your eye, grows bigger in the dark and smaller in bright light.
fact: Glasses bend light a little so that it focuses properly in eyes that are slightly too long or too short; short-sighted people see near things well, long-sighted people see far things well.
fact: Looking straight at the Sun, even with sunglasses, can burn the back of the eye without any pain, and the damage can be permanent.
fact: An eclipse of the Sun is watched only with special eclipse glasses or a pinhole projector.
fact: A pinhole in a box makes an upside-down picture, because light in straight lines crosses at the hole; the first cameras worked like this, and a bigger hole lets in more light but makes the picture blurrier.
fact: A telescope gathers much more light than your eye and makes the picture bigger, and Galileo saw the moons of Jupiter with a small one in 1610.
fact: A microscope uses two lenses, each making the picture bigger again, and shows that a drop of pond water is full of tiny living things.
fact: A laser makes a narrow beam of light of one colour that hardly spreads, and it must never be shone into anyone's eyes or at planes.
fact: Light can travel along thin glass threads, called optical fibres, bouncing inside them, and the internet uses such fibres under the sea.
fact: A mirage of water on a hot road is light from the sky bent by hot air near the road, which is why the puddle vanishes as you get closer.
exercise: Why does a magnifying glass make an ant look huge? | Its curved glass bends the light so the ant looks bigger | It pulls the ant up closer to your eye with a kind of suction | It warms the ant so it swells | It shines extra light on the ant
exercise: Why must you never point a magnifying glass at the Sun? | It can focus sunlight into a spot hot enough to start a fire | It will crack the glass at once | It makes the Sun look too small | It turns the sunlight green
exercise: What does the lens inside your eye do? | It bends light to make a sharp picture on the back of the eye | It stores pictures like a memory card | It gives out light so you can see | It keeps dust out of the eye
exercise: Which way up is the picture on the back of your eye? | Upside down, and the brain turns it right | Exactly the right way up, just like the world | Sideways, turned a quarter of the way round | It changes which way up it is every second
exercise: What happens to your pupil when you walk into a dark room? | It grows bigger to let in more light | It shrinks right down to keep the dark out | It changes colour to help you see in the dark | It stays exactly the same size as before
exercise: How do glasses help people see clearly? | Their lenses bend light so it focuses properly in the eye | They make the world brighter | They make the eyes bigger | They block out all the blue light that makes things look blurry
exercise: A short-sighted person sees which things well without glasses? | Near things | Far things | Only bright things | Only moving things
exercise: Why must you never look straight at the Sun, even with sunglasses? | The eye's lens can focus sunlight and burn the back of the eye | The Sun will make your eyes water for a day | Sunglasses make the Sun brighter | It is only bad luck, not harmful
exercise: How should people watch an eclipse of the Sun? | With special eclipse glasses or a pinhole projector | With ordinary sunglasses | Through a camera or a phone held right up close to the eye | Through a pair of binoculars
exercise: Why is the picture made by a pinhole box upside down? | Light in straight lines crosses at the hole | The inside of the box was fitted upside down | The paper screen turns the light round on itself | Pinholes only let in light reflected from the floor
exercise: What does a telescope do that your eye cannot? | Gathers much more light and makes the picture bigger | Makes its own beam of light to light up the Moon at night | Brings the Moon closer to the Earth | Turns night into day
exercise: What did Galileo see with a small telescope in 1610? | The moons of Jupiter | The rings of Pluto | People on the Moon | The edge of the universe
exercise: What can a microscope show in a drop of pond water? | Tiny living creatures | Nothing but water | Small fish eggs only | Gold dust from the stream
exercise: Why must you never shine a laser pointer at a plane or into someone's eyes? | It can blind and dazzle | It can make the plane fly faster | It can heat the plane up | It is only rude, not harmful
exercise: How does a fibre optic lamp glow at the tips of its strands? | Light bounces along inside the thin glass threads | Each strand has a tiny little bulb hidden at the tip | The strands soak up light from the room | The tips are painted with glow paint
exercise: What is the shimmering "water" you see on a hot road? | Light from the sky bent by hot air near the road | A real puddle of rain that dries up as you come near it | Oil spilled by cars | Steam rising from the tar
test: Why does the "puddle" on a hot road vanish when you get close? | It was the sky's light bent by hot air, not water | It dries up in the time it takes you to walk there | Cars splash it away | It sinks into the road
test: bruv, eclipse today, i'll just wear me sunnies and stare at it yeah? | No, sunglasses are not enough, use eclipse glasses or a pinhole | Yes, sunglasses are fine | Yes, if you only look for an hour | No, but squinting makes it safe
test: In a cartoon a kid zaps ants with a magnifying glass for fun. What is the real rule? | Never point it at the Sun or anything living, it burns | That is a harmless game | It only works on cloudy days | It cools the ants down
test: Why is staring at the Sun extra dangerous, compared with touching something hot? | The damage does not hurt, so you do not know to stop | It hurts so much that you stop at once | It only damages your eyelids | It only harms people who wear glasses or contact lenses
test: Your grandad can read the bus number far away but needs glasses for his book. What is he? | Long-sighted | Short-sighted | Colour-blind | Night-blind
test: What would happen to the pinhole picture if you made the hole bigger? | It would get brighter but blurrier | It would turn round the right way up | It would disappear completely from view | It would become much sharper and clearer
test: Why does a telescope with a bigger lens show fainter stars? | A bigger lens catches more light | A bigger lens brings stars closer | A bigger lens makes stars hotter | A bigger lens shines light on the stars
test: You come out of a dark cinema into bright sunshine and it hurts for a moment. Why? | Your pupils are still wide open and let in too much light | The sunshine got stronger while you were inside | Cinemas make your eyes weaker for ever | The film used up the light in your eyes
test: How does a message cross the ocean on the internet? | As flashes of light inside glass fibres under the sea | By sound sent through the water from one coast to another | By ships carrying the letters | By wind blowing the signal across
test: Which is true about the picture inside your eye? | It is upside down, and the brain turns it the right way | It is the right way up, and the brain then flips it over | It is in black and white only | There is no picture inside the eye
exam: Identify the principal function of the crystalline lens of the human eye. | To refract light so as to form a focused image upon the retina | To emit light whereby the objects before the eye are illuminated at night | To store visual images for subsequent recall | To exclude particles of dust from the eye
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard of observing the Sun directly? | The retina may be permanently injured without any sensation of pain | The injury is invariably accompanied by intense pain | Ordinary sunglasses afford complete protection | No injury can result from brief observation
exam: It is given that a pinhole camera forms an image. Determine the orientation of the image so formed. | Inverted | Upright | Rotated through a quarter turn | Reversed in colour only
exam: Ascertain the nature of the apparent sheet of water observed upon a heated road surface. | Light from the sky refracted by heated air adjacent to the road | Water deposited by passing vehicles | Vapour emitted by the road surface | A reflection of the observer's own shadow
